{"name":"Flannel Pyjamas","hex":"#8b8d98","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flannel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft cloth material originally woven from wool, today often combined with cotton or synthetic fibers.","A washcloth."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of flannel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flannel"},{"term":"pyjamas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["UK standard form of pajamas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pyjamas"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flannel","blocks":[{"text":"Flannel is a soft woven fabric of varying fineness. Flannel was originally made from carded wool or worsted yarn, but is now often made from either wool, cotton, or synthetic fiber. Flannel is commonly used to make tartan clothing, blankets, bed sheets, sleepwear, and several other uses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Flannel_Shirt_For_Abi.jpg/330px-Flannel_Shirt_For_Abi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flannel"},{"title":"Pajamas","blocks":[{"text":"Pajamas or pyjamas, sometimes colloquially shortened to PJs, jammies, jim-jams, or in South Asia, night suits, are several related types of clothing worn as nightwear or while lounging. Pajamas are soft garments derived from the Indian and Iranian bottom-wear, the pyjamas, which were adopted in the Western world as nightwear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Muslim_girl_india1844_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Muslim_girl_india1844_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pajamas"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Washer_Woman_skirt_1957_by_Sybil_Connolly.jpg/500px-Washer_Woman_skirt_1957_by_Sybil_Connolly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=